<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=prosthesis">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Medicine and health</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prosthesis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000–2800 BCE</span> · Also known as prosthetic implant</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A prosthesis is an artificial device that replaces a missing body part lost through injury, disease or a condition present at birth. The earliest archaeological evidence is an eye prosthesis, probably of bitumen paste covered with a thin layer of gold, buried with a woman at Shahr-i Shōkhta in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> about 3000 to 2800 BCE. A wood and leather artificial big toe found on a mummy near Thebes, dated 950 to 710 BCE, shows signs of use. Ambroise Paré, considered the father of modern prosthetics, designed an above-knee device, and functional prostheses appeared in the 1500s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prosthesis.jpg" alt="Prosthesis" width="330" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The U.S. Army · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_The_U.S._Army_-_U.S._Army_World_Class_Athlete_Program_Paralympic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word prosthesis comes from the Ancient Greek prosthesis, meaning &quot;addition, application, attachment&quot;. Prostheses originate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a> around 3000 BCE, with the earliest evidence in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. The earliest recorded mention of eye prostheses is the Egyptian story of the Eye of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a> restores the plucked-out left eye of Horus. The earliest archaeological evidence, from about 3000 to 2800 BCE, is an eye prosthesis buried with a woman at Shahr-i Shōkhta in Iran, probably of bitumen paste covered with a thin layer of gold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A prosthesis may restore the function of a missing body part or serve a cosmetic purpose. The part may be lost through injury, disease or a condition present at birth. Limb prostheses replace arms or legs at various levels of amputation, and organ prostheses include artificial hearts and kidneys. Cosmetic prostheses have long been used to disguise injuries and disfigurements. A prosthetist prescribes, designs and manages the device, usually beginning with a plaster cast of the affected limb.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> were early makers of foot prostheses, as a wooden toe found on a mummy shows. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigveda">Rigveda</a>, about 1200 BCE, tells of the warrior queen Vishpala. Roman bronze crowns have also been found, but their use may have been more aesthetic than medical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>An Italian surgeon recorded an amputee whose artificial arm allowed him to remove his hat, open his purse and sign his name. Ambroise Paré, considered the father of modern prosthetics, improved both amputation surgery and design; among his inventions was an above-knee device, a kneeling peg leg and foot with a fixed position, an adjustable harness and a knee lock. Pieter Verduyn made the first non-locking below-knee prosthesis. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the National Academy of Sciences urged more research, and government funding set up programmes in the armed forces and the Veterans Administration. Elaine Scarry argues that every artifact extends the body, as chairs supplement the skeleton and tools the hands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Prosthesis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3000–2800 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Shahr-i Shōkhta, Iran</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The wood and leather toe from a burial near Thebes, dated 950 to 710 BCE, is described as the first confirmed use of a prosthetic device, as it shows wear; before its discovery in 2000 the oldest known was an artificial leg from Capua.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=prosthesis">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
